tasks/BACKLOG.md:469:🟡 **F-2460-2 — THREE STORY SPECS ARE RED ON MAIN AND NOTHING RECORDED THEM; THE RUNNER NAMED ONE OF THE THREE (s2460, 2026-09-03, PRE-EXISTING, NON-BLOCKING, FIRE-AUTHORABLE).** `ss-01-beats.spec.ts:103`, `ss-03-beats.spec.ts:52` and `story-loop.spec.ts:185` fail on BOTH projects, on main, without this slice. 🔬 **PROVEN BY A CONTROL, not by argument: I re-ran the four adjacent story suites with the slice's `beats.ts` + `StoryRuntime.ts` reverted to main and got the IDENTICAL 6 failures (24 pass / 6 fail merged; 16 pass / 6 fail control).** The runner's report named only story-loop's *"existing reload-helper failure"* and folded the rest into a broad *"unrelated linked-worktree, engine-pin, Node-version, fixture, and simulation checks"* — `ss-01` and `ss-03` appear nowhere in it, so a drain that inherited the report would have mis-attributed two reds. Causes diagnosed rather than guessed: **`ss-03:52`** — the spec's hardcoded `AUTHORED_IDS` list has been outgrown by the ceremony beats, receiving 6 extra ids (`e2-ceremony-valley`, `e2-ceremony-title`, `e3-ceremony-dynamo`, `e3-ceremony-tree`, `e3-ceremony-title`, `e2-railcar-arrival`) — **none is an E3 STORY beat from this slice**, and the three `e3-ceremony-*` are the pre-existing ones the master explicitly required to stay; **`ss-01:103`** — expects the `first-contract` card but the queue yields `ledger-page:town_elder`, a beat-precedence effect of the ledger-page work; **`story-loop:185`** — the reload-helper failure. ➡️ **Small and fire-authorable: `ss-03` is a stale-list fix.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:501:🗺️ **F-2457-4 — L1 ERA MECHANIC AUDIT DRAINED (fire s2457, `9ba05ae60f6fb5398c13358d56ea837c7477dbdc`), AND ITS RESULT IS A LADDER-SCOPE QUESTION FOR THE OWNER.** Every admitted E3–E10 door contract was ridden once through `gr-sim` and judged against its era's signature mechanic: **9 EXERCISES · 3 PARTIAL · 14 RESKIN · 6 UNRIDDEN** over 32 contracts (26 door-admitted; the 6 absent are exactly the 6 `gr-sim` refusals, each preserved verbatim rather than guessed). 🔑 **THE AUDIT REFUTED ITS OWN TASK'S PREMISE, WHICH IS THE MOST VALUABLE THING IN IT: the inherited belief that E4, E8 and E9 have "no engine system" is STALE.** They exist and are cited by file:line — `ConvoyBehavior`, `RoadNetwork`, `WeatherSystem` (E4), `E8PhysicsSystem` (E8), `SeedCaravanSystem`, `CanalChoiceSystem`, `ScheduledRelocationSystem`, `E9CanalSystem` (E9). **The real defect is COMPOSITION:** `HeadlessContractSim` imports and composes none of the E4 transport/weather systems and no `E8PhysicsSystem`, and composes only Low Orbit's projectile-return subset (`src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts:52-83`, `:728-744`); E10 has browser systems with the same headless gap. ⚖️ **SO NOTHING IS BROKEN FOR THE PLAYER — the browser game composes these systems. It is the RIDER that cannot reach them, which means agent reels and bench evidence across most of the middle saga are measuring ordinary survival rather than the ladder mechanic.** That reframes the work the ladder batch was authored against: the cheap fix is to compose the existing systems into the headless rider; the expensive one is to treat the eras as unbuilt. **Which one the saga wants is an owner/spec call, not a fire's — on the desk.** Gates: tsc rc=0 22.6s, build rc=0 75.7s, declared honestly as CONTROL legs since a diff with no code cannot move them; the real evidence is 32 preserved ride logs with wave counts and `eventLogHash` per ride. 🧯 **CUSTODY (Mistake #2, near-miss worth recording): lane-d had ALREADY been refilled when I found this, and the new master's F-1266-1 evidence-artifact exception pardons `artifacts/**` — so a reset would have DISCARDED all 32 ride logs. The work survived only because the runner auto-commits. I pinned `archive/era-mechanic-audit-s2457-1e2ad5473` BEFORE gating, and gated the PINNED COMMIT rather than the moving branch (`main..lane/d` is therefore not expected empty; ancestry verified YES).** The audit also declares its own honesty limit unprompted — `gr-sim` does not serialize the event list, so invocation is judged from rider-visible diagnostics and "no hidden event contents are guessed". Review: `reviews/era-mechanic-audit.md`.
