tasks/BACKLOG.md:1256:🔴 **F-2169-1 (s2169 2026-08-22, ATTENDED-OWED — A SECOND REGRESSION WAS HIDING BEHIND THE STALE PIN F-2167-1 AND F-2168-1 BOTH DIAGNOSED: THE CANYON-WORKS CRAWLER CONTRACT NO LONGER TERMINATES, AND IT IS **NOT** ap16-1's DOING.)** 🎯 **SYMPTOM:** `HeadlessContractSim.outcome()` throws `Outcome requested before the contract terminated.` at `e2e/er01-e3-census.spec.ts:171` (`const outcome = sim.outcome();`, in the `${contract.id} census support is explicit and deterministic` test), inside `runCrawler()`, **after** the test kills all three `dynamo_crawler` boss components (`drain_mast`, `tracks`, `capacitor_bank`). Deterministic, both projects. It is the sole remaining content of `er01-e3-census` after the f2168-1 re-pin merged at `73872b6ac` (4 failed → **2 failed / 6 passed**; `e3-blackout-ridge` fully green). 🔬 **PROVEN *NOT* TO BE THE ap16-1 BLAST RADIUS — THREE CONTROLS, EACH `--workers=1` IN A DETACHED WORKTREE, AND THE MIDDLE ONE IS DECISIVE:** ⑴ at **`d6166b292`** (immediately **pre**-ap16-1), canyon-works **PASSES** (15.4 s) on that tree's own old pins — so `:171` used to execute and the contract used to terminate. ⑵ at **`8465f6b33`** (**ap16-1 itself**), with **the re-pinned spec overlaid**, canyon-works **PASSES** (14.7 s) — *the whole test, including `:171`*. ⑶ on **main + the merge**, it **FAILS**. **Holding the assertion staleness constant and varying only the tree, ap16-1 explains the assertions and NOTHING ELSE.** The termination break entered **after** `8465f6b33` (2026-08-10T19:08). 🧭 **BISECTED TO `d599cd3ea` — *"fix: AP-16-6C — truthful terminal receipts"* (2026-08-12T09:25:55+07)**, using the re-pinned spec overlaid on each candidate tree as the oracle so only sim behaviour varies. Nearest good: `58afad35d` (f1662-2). The commit rewrites **`src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts` (+211 lines)** — the exact file that throws — and its headline is a deliberate change to *when a contract counts as terminated*. Name and symptom agree. ⚠️ **SCOPE OF THE ATTRIBUTION, STATED RATHER THAN IMPLIED:** the bisect sampled the **61 first-parent commits touching `src/sim`/`src/systems`/`src/entities`/`assets/contracts`** out of **1904** in the window — not all 1904, and not second parents. `d599cd3ea` is a **merge commit** (`git show -- <path>` prints nothing for it; use `git diff d599cd3ea^ d599cd3ea`), so the true first-bad may be one of its own children. **The line-level mechanism is `UNVERIFIED`** — what is verified is pre-ap16-1 PASS, at-ap16-1 PASS, today FAIL, first sampled bad commit rewrites the throwing file. ⚖️ **WHY THIS IS ATTENDED-OWED AND NOT FIRE-AUTHORABLE:** "truthful terminal receipts" was a *deliberate tightening* of termination semantics. Either the census is asserting an outcome the new receipts law no longer grants (fix the test) or the crawler genuinely fails to terminate in play (fix the sim) — and choosing between those is a design fork on the sim's terminal contract. 🚫 **DO NOT CURE THIS BY RE-PINNING `:171`.** That is the F-1441-3 prohibition (re-pin only with a named cause, never to make a red go away) and it would bury a real gameplay break for the **second** time. Rule it in the same sitting as F-2165-1 / F-2168-1. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF — A RECURRENCE OF THE LAW F-2168-1 ITSELF WROTE DOWN, ONE LEVEL DEEPER.** s2168 correctly refuted F-2167-1's claim that certain assertions "still pass" by observing they *never execute*. **Neither fire could see that the SAME masking was hiding a *behavioural* regression 120 lines further down the same test**, because nothing behind a failing `expect` runs. ➡️ **A red suite's failure count is a FLOOR on the number of defects, never the count. When a re-pin cures an assertion, EXPECT MORE FAILURES TO APPEAR, NOT FEWER — and treat each newly-reached one as a fresh subject with its own cause, not as residue of the cause you just fixed.** This is also why the runner's `STOPPED` was correct behaviour and its master's tripwire was well-designed: the banked arrays matched exactly, so the stop was not a re-pin failure but a genuinely new subject surfacing.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1260:🔴 **F-2167-1 (s2167 2026-08-22, ATTENDED-OWED, MEASURED NOT INHERITED — TWO ER-01 CENSUS SPECS HAVE BEEN RED ON CLEAN MAIN FOR 12 DAYS, ONE CAUSE, THREE ASSERTION SITES, AND THE DRAIN THAT CAUSED IT RECORDED THE CASUALTY AS A 🟢 GREEN FINDING.)** 📊 **THE RED, RE-MEASURED THIS FIRE ON CLEAN MAIN (`82d6e83ff`, tree verified free of `src`/`e2e`/`assets`/`scripts`/`functions` dirt before running), `--workers=1` per §3.1:** `e2e/er01-e10-census.spec.ts` **8 failed / 0 passed** (4 contracts × 2 projects) and `e2e/er01-e3-census.spec.ts` **4 failed / 4 passed** — **12 failures total.** 🔬 **ONE CAUSE, AND I FINGERPRINTED BY LINE RATHER THAN BY TITLE, WHICH IS THE ONLY REASON THIS IS RIGHT (the s2165 warning, honoured):** the e3 half fails at **TWO DIFFERENT LINES** — `e3-canyon-works` at `:51` `expect(mechanics.buildables).toBeUndefined()`, `e3-blackout-ridge` at `:190` `expect(mechanics).toMatchObject({…})` — while `e3-moth-season` and `e3-fairground` **PASS**. A title-level read would have reported "e3 census red, same cause as e10" and been **half wrong**; the `:190` site is the same root cause reached through a different assertion (its expected `buildables` array gained the six registry entries), but that is a measured result, not an assumption. All e10 failures are at `:59`. 🎯 **ROOT CAUSE, ISOLATED BY `git log -L` ON THE EXACT LINES:** `8465f6b33` (2026-08-10T19:08+07, *"feat: ap16-1 buildable parity"*, runner commit `0dda53a5b`, drained by **s1636**) rewrote `src/agent/MechanicsManifest.ts:693`. **BEFORE:** `const registryBuildables = listContracts().some(({ id }) => id === contract.id) ? ['sentry_beacon','turret'].map(…) : []` — gated on membership, and `listContracts()` defaults to `DEFAULT_EPOCH_ID = 'epoch-1-frontier'` (`src/meta/ContractFamilies.ts:843`), so every non-epoch-1 contract got `[]`. **AFTER:** an **unconditional** six-item literal `['sentry_beacon','palisade','sluice','stockpile','turret','assay_office']` with no membership gate at all. Since the manifest emits the key only when `buildables.length > 0` (`:720`), **`mechanics.buildables` is now structurally incapable of being `undefined` for ANY contract** — the assertion is unsatisfiable, not merely stale. ⚠️ **THE SPECS WERE GREEN WHEN WRITTEN AND WERE BROKEN FROM OUTSIDE:** `er01-e10-census.spec.ts` was born `100d7b843` **2026-08-06**, four days BEFORE the regression. 🩹 **AND THE EXPENSIVE PART — THE CASUALTY WAS SEEN, NAMED, AND FILED AS A GREEN.** `reviews/ap16-1-buildable-parity.md:105` reads, verbatim: *"Its second-opinion review flagged \"old E3 census expectations\"; it left those assertions untouched and said so. That is the reject-don't-stretch convention working (Mistake #14)."* — recorded as **🟢 F-1636-3, "the runner declined an out-of-scope change, correctly."** ✅ **THE RUNNER WAS RIGHT AND THE REVIEW'S PRAISE WAS RIGHT: refusing to edit another slice's census inside a firewall is exactly the convention working.** ❌ **WHAT NOBODY DID WAS RUN THE SPEC AFTERWARDS.** "Left the assertions untouched" and "left a red on main" are the same act described from two sides, and only the flattering side reached the ledger — where a 🟢 reads as *resolved* and stops the next reader looking. **E10 was never flagged at all** (the second-opinion caught e3 only; `grep -i e10 reviews/ap16-1-buildable-parity.md` = 0 hits), so 8 of the 12 failures went unnamed for 12 days. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT IS A RECURRENCE, NOT A NEW SHAPE:** this is the F-1460-1 class one turn further out — *a slice-local spec is structurally incapable of seeing a cross-cutting change* — and the adjacent-suite list was scoped to **the files the master TOUCHED** rather than **the behaviour it CHANGED**. `MechanicsManifest.ts` is a derivation shared by every contract census, so a parity change to it is cross-cutting by construction while touching one file. ➡️ **A DECISION MARKED "declined, out of scope" IS AN OWED MEASUREMENT, NOT A CLOSED ITEM: when a runner correctly refuses to fix a suite it just invalidated, the DRAIN owes that suite a run, and the finding stays 🔺 until someone reports its colour.** ⚖️ **NOT CURED, AND DELIBERATELY NOT AUTHORED — THE REASONING RECORDED RATHER THAN THE GAP HIDDEN (§2E HARD LIMIT).** The 12 failures split into two halves with **different owners**: (1) **A STALE PIN** — `e3-blackout-ridge` is an admitted contract that legitimately gained the core registry, so `:190` is out-of-date bookkeeping the ap16-1 drain owed; re-pinning it is pure mechanics. (2) **A SUBSTANTIVE CLAIM** — the four e10 contracts are ones the sim **REFUSES** (the same spec asserts `new HeadlessContractSim(…)` throws `AP-07 supports only .*received <id>`, and those assertions still pass), yet the manifest now advertises **six BUILD operations** for them. On an agent-facing versioned surface that is a **false capability claim**, and the census encodes the very distinction ap16-1 erased — the spec expects `undefined` for `canyon-works` and a populated array for `blackout-ridge`, in one file. 🚫 **I did not author a corrective, because the correct predicate ("gate on the set the sim will actually admit") lands directly inside the OPEN `F-2165-1` fork over whether e10 is admitted at all** — under its option (b) the manifest's claim becomes TRUE and only the pin needs updating; under (a)/(c) the gate must be restored. **A master written today could not reach green inside any firewall that does not pre-empt that ruling** — which is precisely the jointly-unsatisfiable defect ap16-1's own review recorded as F-1636-2. **This rides with F-2165-1 to the same attended session; it is NOT a new owner question and adds nothing to the desk.**
