tasks/BACKLOG.md:1516:✅ **F-2096-1 CURED — THE SAME-GAME REPORT GUARD WAS WATCHING THE STABLE HALF, AND MAIN CARRIED A ROW CALLING A CLOSED FINDING "PENDING" (s2096, cure `780a5c990`, regeneration `b09a013a6`).** Found while judging the GZ-01 candidate `e2b9399a2` by reading its code rather than its headline: that merge rewrote the `e3-fairground` `reason:` string in `CONTRACT_ADMISSION_EXEMPTIONS` to record F-E3CF-4 as CLOSED, but `docs/bench/same-game-audit.md` is GENERATED from those same values (`scripts/same-game-audit.mjs:420` emits `cell(entry.reason)`) and was not regenerated — so main's committed report said *"Admission HELD **pending** a public-verb secure x2 per seed"* for a reason source declares closed. **A row asserting a finding is pending when source says closed is worse than a missing row: it is read as current.** ⚠️ **THE GUARD BUILT FOR EXACTLY THIS COULD NOT SEE IT, AND ITS OWN HEADER SAYS SO WHILE BEING WRONG ABOUT ITS REACH:** `scripts/same-game-report-guard.test.mjs:14–15` reads *"The source-set check is what catches a report nobody regenerated"* — but that check compares only `tableIds.sort()` against `Object.keys(CONTRACT_ADMISSION_EXEMPTIONS)`, i.e. **the KEY SET, leaving every VALUE in the table unguarded.** The id set is the LEAST volatile thing in that table — ids move only when a contract is admitted or exempted (rare, ratcheted), reasons move whenever anyone measures anything (often). 🔬 **PROVEN BY MANUFACTURING THE DEFECT, NOT BY A GREEN (the s1299/s1300 standard): restoring the pre-merge reason to line 57 leaves BOTH pre-existing tests GREEN (`✔ prose matches its table`, `✔ table matches source`) while the new assertion REDS** with `stale exemption reason for 'e3-fairground'`; report restored and verified by `git diff --quiet`. **CURE, both halves:** (1) regenerated on the merged tree — **872 changed lines, classified before writing: 871 pure coordinate rot (`File.ts:NNN` drift accumulated from many merges) + exactly 1 semantic, the row above**; the generator is cheap (5.1s, no sims) so staleness here was never a cost question. (2) The guard now asserts each row's `reason` and `citation` against source through the same `cell()` escaping, with the regeneration command in the failure message. 💡 **CLASS — the third sibling of F-1660-1 and F-1662-2, and the sharpest statement of it yet: those were *a policy encoded as a comment evaporates when its data structure is derived* and *a count encoded as prose rots when its table grows*. This one is A GUARD AIMED AT THE PART OF ITS SUBJECT THAT DOES NOT MOVE. All three stayed green for the same reason — nothing asserted the prose against the data — but this one had a guard whose stated purpose was that assertion, which is why it went unexamined the longest.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1518:🔺 **F-2086-1 — THE SANCTIONED CENSUS HARNESS CANNOT DRIVE ANY E3 CONTRACT, SO NO E3 CENSUS CAN BE RUN AT ALL (measured s2086, drained s2087 `bd6334c35`).** `scripts/gr-sim-campaign.mjs` filters its board to `contract.id === 'the-claim' || contract.id.startsWith('e1-')` and takes **no contract argument** (its parser accepts only player/output/checkpoint/resume/test-fixture) — **VERIFIED at drain time by reading the file, not inherited.** So the prescribed census command silently measures early E1 contracts instead of the one asked for. ⚠️ **This is a CLASS blocker, not one task's bad luck:** every future E2–E10 census slice hits it, and the failure mode is the dangerous one — the harness **succeeds** and returns numbers about the wrong contracts. 💡 **The s2086 run did the right thing and it is worth naming:** it refused to substitute a custom runner, because a number from an unsanctioned harness is inadmissible, and stopped with its fixture-shaped player preserved at `scripts/f2086-canyon-census-player.mjs`. **RECORDED-NOT-BUILT:** widening the harness needs either a `--contract` argument or a relaxed board filter; both edit an existing sanctioned script, which that task's firewall forbade. **GATE: an E3+ census slice is not authorable until the harness can select its contract.** What the run DID establish from source (all re-verified at drain): `required: 2` counts powered `consumer`/`gallery` graph nodes; three series pylon relays feed each of the two galleries, so both branches need all **six** beacons; the six-beacon chain costs **330 g** (`25+35+45+55+75+95` under `ceil(x/5)*5` from `Balance.beacon` costBase 25 / growth 1.3 / maxCount 6 — recomputed exactly); `byWave: 6` compares the wave index, not elapsed seconds. What it does NOT establish, stated plainly in its own report: no census result, no margin, no `eventLogHash`, no reachability verdict, no balance recommendation, and it neither reproduces nor disputes the earlier `212.77 s` vs `210.03 s` figure. Evidence `artifacts/f2086-canyon-census/REPORT.md`, machine-readable `census.json` (empty `rows`, blocked reason). ✅ **GATE OPEN — CURED AND MERGED s2122 `ce75bc31970404a9a48c8e94a70c542739739112`** (slice `f2120-1`, review `reviews/f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select.md`). The harness now takes `--contract <id>` and **refuses loudly** on unknown / unscored-practice / unseeded / locked ids, each exiting non-zero **above the `mkdir`**, so the dangerous arm this row named — *"the harness **succeeds** and returns numbers about the wrong contracts"* — cannot occur. The default board is provably unchanged: the pre-existing control `scripts/gr-sim-campaign.test.mjs:62` still pins `fnv1a32:4f363fd5` and the five-leg order, unmodified. **E3+ census slices are fire-authorable from here**, with one condition this row could not have known: the drain answered the slice's resolve-and-report item **YES** — `--resume` CAN supply an epoch-3-active profile with no unlock bypass (`scripts/gr-sim-campaign.mjs:55-60` → `src/game/ProfileTransfer.ts:64-76`/`:136-150` → `src/meta/ContractFamilies.ts:1091-1108`), so an E3 census needs a **genuine epoch-3 checkpoint**, not merely the new argument. A fresh checkpoint stays correctly locked.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1519:🔬 **F-2120-1 (s2120 2026-08-21, DRY BOARD — THE BOARD WAS CALLED DRY FOR 33 MORE FIRES WHILE A CLASS-BLOCKING, FIRE-AUTHORABLE CURE SAT IN A `RECORDED-NOT-BUILT` ROW. F-2086-2 NAMED THIS EXACT CLASS AND ITS CURE SWEPT ONE ROW OF THE FIRST DENOMINATOR ONLY.)** 📋 **THE INHERITED CLAIM.** s2115–s2119 each handed forward **PIPELINE-DRY: lanes a, c, d**, two of them citing `authorable-candidates` at **0 fire-authorable**. I re-ran it and **confirmed the number exactly**: 12 planned leaves, 12 priced, 0 authorable (owner-gated 7, attended-owed 3, prose-only 1, needs-spec 1) — and every price it cites is dated **2026-08-11**, ten days stale. ⚠️ **THE NUMBER IS RIGHT AND THE CONCLUSION IS WRONG, FOR THE SECOND TIME, BY THE SAME MECHANISM.** **F-2086-2** (s2086) already measured this: the instrument’s denominator is **planned leaves**, and it closed with the reusable half — *“the desk, the findings rows and the specs are three further denominators”*. **Its cure took ONE row from the FIRST denominator (the desk, `F-E2S-4`) and the other two were never swept.** A later handoff even carried the lesson forward as an instruction — *“price a lane against those before declaring PIPELINE-DRY”* — and **33 consecutive fires then re-declared PIPELINE-DRY citing only the planned-leaves tool.** That is the F-2115-1 shape exactly: a row naming its own next command, which never ran. ✅ **WHAT THE SECOND DENOMINATOR ACTUALLY HELD.** Sweeping the **findings rows**, `F-2086-1` is a 🔺 row carrying an explicit **`RECORDED-NOT-BUILT`** clause that names its own cure verbatim — *“widening the harness needs either a `--contract` argument or a relaxed board filter; both edit an existing sanctioned script, which that task’s firewall forbade”* — under a standing **`GATE: an E3+ census slice is not authorable until the harness can select its contract.`** It is a **CLASS blocker in its own words** (*“every future E2–E10 census slice hits it”*) whose failure mode is the dangerous one (*“the harness **succeeds** and returns numbers about the wrong contracts”*). It is instrument-only, zero product change, reversible — **fire-authorable on every axis, and it sat unbuilt for 33 fires.** 🔎 **PREMISE RE-VERIFIED AT SOURCE, NOT INHERITED (the §0.4 duty):** the filter is live at `scripts/gr-sim-campaign.mjs:67-68` (`the-claim || e1-*`); the parser at `:186` still accepts only `player/output/checkpoint/resume/test-fixture`; and `git log` on that file shows **exactly one commit ever** (`9849623b6`, the original E1 build) — so nothing has touched it since the finding. Mistake #8 checked: no master targets the cure, no done-move matches. 🎯 **AND THE FINDING’S OWN CURE SKETCH IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT — FOUND BY READING, AND FOLDED INTO THE MASTER SO IT CANNOT BURN THE RUN. Three gates stand between the filter and an E3 number; F-2086-1 named only the first.** (1) the board filter `:67-68`; (2) the **unlock gate** `:73` → `contractUnlockStatus`, which returns `unlocked:false` for any inactive epoch (`src/meta/ContractUnlock.ts:22`), and `epochIsActive` is `active.order >= requested.order` (`src/meta/ContractFamilies.ts:1111-1115`) with a fresh profile at epoch-1 — so **`e3-canyon-works` is LOCKED by construction**; (3) the **campaign-walk loop** `:71-74`, a `while(true)` that `break`s when no candidate is unlocked, so a widened filter alone **exits having measured nothing** — the same silent-wrong-answer failure, merely relocated. The bench-seed constraint at `:78-79` is **not** a blocker: `e3-canyon-works` is seeded with both seeds (34 contracts seeded, all four E3s). ➡️ **DISPATCHED** as `tasks/f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select.md` (**lane-a**, leaf `f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select`), instrument-only: firewalled off `src/**`, `assets/contracts/**` and **any unlock bypass**, with the three refusal arms (unknown / unseeded / locked) required to exit **non-zero before any sim starts** and the locked arm required to print `contractUnlockStatus`’s own `condition` text. Backward compatibility is held by an **existing** control the master forbids editing — `scripts/gr-sim-campaign.test.mjs:62` (“campaign walks E1 legally, persists each leg, and hashes deterministically”). Scope item 5 is deliberately **resolve-and-REPORT**: whether `--resume` can supply an E3-active profile is answered in writing, because forcing a locked contract to run is a sim-semantics call with canon reach and is not a fire’s to take. Lane currency verified per F-1320-2: `lane/a` contains `9849623b6` and its copy of the harness is **byte-identical to main**. 💡 **THE REUSABLE HALF, AND IT IS A SHARPENING OF F-2086-2 RATHER THAN A REPEAT: when a finding names a class of denominators, sweeping ONE member is not sweeping the class — and the row that records the partial sweep will read like a completed one.** F-2086-2’s closing sentence is correct and was carried forward faithfully as prose by every fire since; what none of them did was **execute it against the second denominator**. ⚠️ **A `RECORDED-NOT-BUILT` clause is the highest-value thing on the board for an idle fire and the easiest to miss, because it looks like a finished finding: it is a 🔺 row, it has a hash, it is drained, and its work is entirely undone.** Grep the findings rows for `RECORDED-NOT-BUILT` and `GATE:` before ever writing PIPELINE-DRY again. ✅ **SHIPPED s2122 `ce75bc31970404a9a48c8e94a70c542739739112`** — drained by the s2122 fire; review `reviews/f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select.md`, leaf `f2120-1-campaign-harness-contract-select` → `merged`. **The runner delivered all six scope items and added a fourth refusal arm that the master's own ⚠️ clause required but its numbered list omitted** (`practice.scores === false`, which would otherwise have filtered to an empty board and exited 0 having measured nothing); its independent `codex review` also caught `--contract=` falling through to the default board, now pinned by a sixth test. Gates on the merged tree in a detached `gate-s2122` worktree (§3.0b custody): tsc rc=0 · build rc=0 · own spec **6/6** · `fixture-teardown` **34 subjects** rc=0 · `function-cors-allowlist` 2/2 · `gate-caller-audit` 26/26 · all three named refusal arms **reproduced verbatim by the drain's own control run**, exit 1 each. ⚠️ **Two findings filed, both non-blocking and both worth reading before the next E3 slice.** **F-2122-1**: the merge **rotted a `src/**` line citation** — `src/sim/HeadlessContractSim.ts:715` cited `gr-sim-campaign.mjs:88` for the profile-storage write site, which the slice's +15 lines moved to `:103`, leaving `:88` on an unrelated `if (!contract) break;`. Re-based in the landing commit `11256af3c` per the `CLAUDE.md` §4.10b lifecycle and re-phrased to name the CODE at the site. **The class is the point: `law-pointer-guard` watches law surfaces only, so a `src/**` comment citing a `scripts/**` line rots SILENTLY and forever.** No guard proposed — one over every backticked `file:line` in `src/` comments would red constantly and earn a permanent excuse (the `cross-engine` fate, F-1460-1). **F-2122-2**: the **full `test:node-guards` battery did NOT run on the merged tree**, and the reason is machine arrangement, not judgement — a concurrent attended six-file battery (pid 40448) plus a five-way playwright chromium fleet took 1-min load from **18 → 78 → 362** and held it at **241–362**. Two disqualifying effects: `node-guards-contention` reds by design on a detected second battery (a **false red about my own drain** — the same predeclared red the runner hit in-lane), and F-2076-1 measured per-test budget margins moving **2.0–2.4× with load**. I ran the **measured dependency surface** instead — `grep -rln gr-sim-campaign` returns exactly five hits, of which the only code dependents are the own test and `function-cors-allowlist` (both green), plus `fixture-teardown` because the diff adds a new `mkdtemp` prefix. ➡️ **OWED, one command on a quiet machine: `node scripts/run-guards.mjs --changed-since 7fd7d44fc4ed3d88f0ec050e8ae9ae0181bda716`.** ✅ **DISCHARGED IN THE SAME FIRE (s2122) — DO NOT CARRY THIS FORWARD.** The machine cleared ~20 min after the merge (lane-c finished, the playwright fleet drained, 1-min load **362 → 7.5**) and the command was run on merged main: `test:node-guards` **PASS rc=0 709s** · `test:power-budget` PASS `p95=0.481ms` · `test:task-guards` PASS · `test:citations` PASS · `test:gate-callers` PASS — **5/5**, 228 files changed since base, base gate only (no path rule matched). **So the merge is fully gated, and `node-guards-contention` — the arm that would have produced a false red about this very drain — passes when asked on a quiet machine**, confirming the finding's own diagnosis that the runner's predeclared in-lane red was the arrangement's and not the slice's. ⏱️ **One number recorded and one conclusion REFUSED: 709s against the 404.7s `scripts/fire.md` §3.1 records from s2099 — a 1.75× spread. DO NOT re-pin the law's cost figure from it.** s2099 measured at load **3.75**; this ran from load **~13** with a playwright tail still draining, and F-2076-1 already established that per-test margins move **2.0–2.4× with load**. **That is a measurement about the MACHINE, not the battery** — re-pinning a law figure from it is the make-the-number-go-away reflex the pin site prohibits. Practical budget: **7–12 min, run ALONE.** **F-2122-3** (carried, non-blocking): the master's LANE CURRENCY note claimed `lane/a` held two lane-only E7 bench seeds; the runner reports **neither is present** and correctly reported rather than fixing, its firewall forbidding `assets/contracts/**`. Nothing in this merge depends on them; worth one grep by whoever next authors against `bench-seeds.json`. 💡 **The reusable half of the DRAIN, distinct from the authoring lesson above: when the prescribed battery is unavailable, the honest substitute is a MEASURED dependency surface, not a smaller battery chosen by feel — and the gap must be named in the review, the ledger and the merge message, or a targeted green reads forever after as a full sweep.**
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1520:✅🎪 **E3-FAIRGROUND ADMITTED — F-E3CF-4 CLOSED BY OPENING THE DOOR, AND THE FIX WAS THE MAP'S OWN GROUND (2026-08-21, owner ruling verbatim: "lets follow your recommendation" → AUTHOR THE ANCHOR SET; agent `worktree-agent-a932151866f4f456e`).** 🔑 **THE ONE THING FOUR SESSIONS NEVER TOUCHED:** `e3-fairground` had **never had `harvestAnchors` of its own** — it inherited `Terrain.DEFAULT_NODE_ANCHORS`, whose nearest live seam sits **38wu out on bench seed 01 and 46wu on seed 02**. That single fact was the residual behind every earlier refusal: the opening purse arrived after the first saboteur, and the Fair Wheel's dynamo (which stops for the whole run on its FIRST hit) was already stopped. Not the escort. Not the consumer. The ground. 📍 **SIX ANCHORS AUTHORED AT 17–24wu**, the placement its admitted siblings already use (`e3-blackout-ridge`: three at 7–16wu): `(±10,−16)` 17.2 in the 10wu-clear band between the crowd lanes · `(±18,−38)` 19.7 and `(±24,−34)` 24.3, all south of z=−30 where **no crowd ever walks**. Two properties do the work: **every** anchor is inside the fair opening radius, so it no longer matters which pair the seed's shuffle activates (that asymmetry WAS the seed-02 residual), and **none** sits in the middle crowd's corridor. Seam count unchanged at six — supply identical, only the geography moved. Mask table + `e3-mask-tables` key list updated in the same commit, so the map is held to the sibling standard. ✅ **THE PROOF, THROUGH THE PLAIN PUBLIC DOOR (no `admissionProbe`), ×2 per seed, byte-identical repeats: seed 01 `fnv1a32:7a66c50b` and seed 02 `fnv1a32:86c9ca37`, BOTH wave 12, wheel spinning, ALL THREE CROWDS ACROSS ([4,1,6] and [2,1,4]), 630/560 gold panned, ZERO granted.** The probe path returns **the same two hashes** — which is itself the proof the seam was never doing anything. 🔧 **One rider change came with the new ground, the mirror of the old one:** the ten-gold opening exemption existed because waiting for a full purse at a 38–46wu seam meant dying first; at 17wu the same rule became a leak (measured: dribbled every ten coins into new wall, gold=0 from t=8, six works decayed to three by t=26). Past the opening, an errand must be worth the walk. 📊 **ADMISSION SURFACES MOVED AND EVERY NUMBER IS ATTRIBUTED:** exemption row OUT (6→5) · census branch flipped to **ADMITTED** with the ADMISSION MOVE comment and the ruling verbatim, asserting the ordinary door OPENING · skill.md fence + door baseline take the id IN (26→27) · **floors regenerated, `--check` clean at 61** (was 59 — the fairground's two rows returned) and **LAW 2 HOLDS: `-01` wave 1 `fnv1a32:647fa993`, `-02` wave 1 `fnv1a32:4bbf4662`, both `secured:false`, and no floor anywhere is `secured:true`** — an idle fair still loses · **audit `agent-lacks 402→383 · equal 878→897 · not-offered 10 · exemptions 6→5 · rows UNCHANGED at 1290`**, and **ATTRIBUTED BY REVERT-AND-REPRODUCE: putting the row back reproduced `6 / 402 / 878 / 10 / 1290` to the digit.** The move is worth **exactly NINETEEN rows** flipping `agent-lacks`→`equal` — **precisely the shape the review recorded in advance while the hold was on** ("one contract's worth of movement, 19 rows on its base"), measured then in both directions and now paid out. 🧾 **GATES:** tsc + build rc=0 · flocks + fairground **6/6 both projects, assertions unmodified** · census 4 pass / 4 fail (the same inventoried blackout-ridge/canyon-works reds, fingerprint-matched) · **node guards 44/44** incl. the door ratchet, the skill.md guard's positive control, the audit pin and the mask tables · floors `--check` clean. **Voltage is now 4/4 on the door.** Review: `reviews/e3-fairground-crowd-flocks.md`.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1522:🛑✅ **F-E3CF-4 CLOSED — THE FAIRGROUND RESTS AS A DOCUMENTED, BENCHMARK-EXEMPT MAP (2026-08-21, agent `worktree-agent-a932151866f4f456e`; the hold is now PERMANENT for this programme).** Three changed-premise attempts and two owner rulings, all measured, all preserved. **① FUNDING** — `prover-v2.mjs` funds the whole fort by panning: **seed 01 SECURED ×2 `fnv1a32:9f2740ab`** (wave 12, crossings [4,1,3], 800 panned / 619 spent / **0 granted**), seed 02 ×2 `fnv1a32:afdd8c2a` at the ceiling with the wheel still turning. **② GEOMETRY** — the ruled 6wu lane shift measured **strictly worse** on one instrument ([5,3,7]/[4,4,3] → [5,0,7]/[4,0,5]) and ships at 0. **③ FORT SHAPE** — after the ruled spawn move, `prover-v3.mjs` designs its fort from a measured pressure map (south corners 65% of all damage from t=13; north corners 26%/16% from t=95; **nothing at all comes for a beacon**, 0–1%, first hit t=287) — **×2 both seeds, byte-identical: seed 01 `fnv1a32:8e1ea01a` wave 14, wheel intact, 650 panned, crossings [4,0,4]; seed 02 `fnv1a32:fa8ab8b1` wave 3.** Six opening variants landed within one wave of each other. 🔍 **THE FINDING THAT EXPLAINS THE WHOLE ARC (F-E3CF-6):** v2's securing fort was an ACCIDENT — two beacon spots one unit from the ring's own flank post that `place_building` can never accept, stopping the purse reserve at ~14 works and leaving the rest for upkeep. **An accidental shape does not travel**, and a deliberate one does not reproduce it, because the pressure map says where damage lands on a COMPLETE fort, not what to buy first (ordering the 65% corners first killed both seeds by wave 3 — they are the ground furthest from the seam field the purse comes from). 📌 **THE RESIDUAL, PRECISELY:** seed 02's first live seam is 46wu out, so its opening purse lands ~5s later than seed 01's, and that margin IS the wheel race; every fix for the escort left the opening untouched and every fix for the opening cost the fort. **What would change the answer is contract data or a ruling, not rider work:** a `secureWave` below 12, a seam anchor within ~30wu of the stake, or accepting the fairground as an elite map. ⏸️ **ADMISSION SURFACES UNCHANGED THROUGHOUT** — census refusal-first, door baseline without the id, skill.md fence, floors with the fairground rows out. The **exemption row's reason now records the full programme** rather than the first attempt's snapshot (surgical, that row only); audit regenerated and **RE-MEASURED IDENTICAL to HEAD — equal 28 · divergence 4 · not-offered 10, 6 cited exemptions, 1318 rows** — the only churn is line-number refresh, which is the proof a reason string moved nothing. Review carries the terminal state: `reviews/e3-fairground-crowd-flocks.md`.
tasks/BACKLOG.md:1524:🎯⏸️ **"MOVE THE SPAWN" — RULED, LANDED, AND IT WORKS ON THE MAP WHILE THE RIDER DOES NOT YET FUND IT (2026-08-20, owner ruling verbatim: "move the spawn"; agent worktree `worktree-agent-a932151866f4f456e`).** 📍 **WHERE THE SPAWN LIVED:** engine-derived, never contract data — `WaveSystem.ts:557` `Balance.waves.spawnRingRadius` (26) and `:562` `edge 'north' → (center.x + lateral, center.z + radius)` off `territoryRingSpawnCenter → heroPosition (0,-30)`, i.e. **(0,-4.1) exactly as measured**. 🔌 **THE SEAM USED — AND THE ONE DELIBERATELY REFUSED:** `tileParams.noSpawnZones` was the obvious lever and is **forbidden**: `ContractFamilies.ts:705` declares it *"Birth-loader output only (tile-persistence sim entries) — never authored in contract JSON"* and `e2e/tp02-green-waypoint.spec.ts:200` (“same seed + same snapshot = identical event logs; the waypoint diverges at tick 0 only and deterministically”) guards that law, so authoring it would have broken an invariant to fix a map. The fix instead rides `ContractEnemyVariant.spawnGates` (`ContractFamilies.ts:373/381` → `WaveSystem.applyVariantSpawnGate:576`) — **authored per VARIANT and per EDGE, already carried by 16 entries across four epochs, two of them in this same epoch file.** So the change is one line of fairground contract data on `night_runner`'s north entry: **no shared spawn code touched, no other map able to inherit it** (F-1471-1 keying, on the variant). Mask table updated in the same commit because `scripts/e3-mask-tables.test.mjs:246` deep-equals the published gates against the roster — guard **30/30 green**. 📐 **THE DECAY TABLE THAT PICKED THE OFFSET:** a runner entering at (X,−4) converges on the stake, so its clearance from the middle lane at height z is |X|×(z+30)/26 → at **−12**: 12.0 at the entry, 9.2 at z=−10, 7.4 at z=−14, covering the lane's swept length down to where the fort's own guns reach; and −12 is the largest offset that still leaves **8wu to the flank crowd's lane at x=−20**, so the fix does not move the problem onto a different crowd (−13 buys 0.6wu more and spends the flank's entire margin). 📊 **MEASURED ON THE SAME FREE-FORT INSTRUMENT (radius-8 ring + 4 beacons, 1 g/s repairs, both seeds), five gates:** baseline (0,−4) [5,**3**,7]/[4,**4**,3] frights 29/33 · **(−12,−4) [4,10,7]/[3,8,3] frights 22/29** · (+12,−4) [8,9,6]/[5,6,1] · (−8,−4) [8,7,7]/[5,7,3] · (+8,−4) [8,4,7]/[6,4,3] — every one secures both seeds, and **the middle crowd goes from 3 and 4 crossings to 10 and 8 at −12, with total frights FALLING on both seeds**, which is the mechanism showing itself. +12 is nearly as good on seed 01 but costs the east flank crowd (1 crossing on seed 02), so the west shoulder wins. ⚠️ **AND THE PUBLIC-VERB PROVER REGRESSED ON IT — F-E3CF-6 CAME DUE, EXACTLY AS THE ROW PREDICTED.** prover-v2 old gate: seed 01 **SECURED w12** `fnv1a32:9f2740ab` · seed 02 w14 `fnv1a32:afdd8c2a`. New gate: seed 01 **w5** `fnv1a32:f0ec6b1e` · seed 02 **w4** `fnv1a32:31392949`; gold panned collapsed 800→200 and 705→175; the wheel survived both times, the hero did not; and **the middle crowd DID cross for the rider on seed 02 ([0,1,0]) — the escort half of the fix works.** Four re-tunes (beacons 4/6 × maxWorks 14/22 × minTrip 45/70) did not recover it (best: seed 01 w14 [4,0,2] at 625 panned). **Cause is the one already on the books:** the securing fort was ACCIDENT-SHAPED — two unplaceable beacon spots capped construction at ~14 works and left the rest of the purse for upkeep — and moving the runners off the lane changed which walls take the pressure. **An accidental shape does not travel.** ⏸️ **SO THE HOLD STANDS AND NOTHING IS CLAIMED THAT IS NOT PROVEN:** exemption row, census refusal branch, door baseline and floors all **untouched**; the map change is kept because the controlled instrument says it strictly improves the ratified objective. ➡️ **The successor is now a RIDER task, not a design one:** fund the fort on the new flow — the free-fort probe say